Stream session cleanup can be starved by cleanup admission
Link: https://chatgpt.com/codex/cloud/security/findings/5cbcff5da48081919566e812c1d8c951?sev=&repo=https%3A%2F%2Fgithub.com%2Fjustincormack%2Fargmin
Criticality: medium (attack path: medium)
Status: fixed

# Resolution
Fixed in commit [`cd33dda2`](https://github.com/justincormack/argmin/commit/cd33dda2) (`Split background cleanup admission lanes`).

The fix removes stream-session scavenging from the shared single-slot `DurableCleanup` admission class. Background cleanup admission is now split into separate fixed nonzero lanes: `ReclaimCleanup` for object payload reclaim and bucket-delete finalization, `LifecycleCleanup` for lifecycle sweeps, and `StreamSessionCleanup` for abandoned stream-session cleanup. `StreamSessionCleanup` has its own counter and limit, so reclaim or lifecycle work can no longer consume the only permit needed for stale stream-session scavenging.

The UAT metrics summary now reports `stream_session_cleanup` independently from `reclaim_cleanup` and `lifecycle_cleanup`, so admission, denial, and finish behavior for stream-session cleanup can be observed directly.

Verification for the fix:
- `cargo fmt`
- `cargo test -p server-core background_work`
- `bash -n scripts/uat-s3-tests`
- `cargo clippy --all-targets --all-features -- -D warnings`
- `cargo nextest run`

