# Summary
Introduced: the stream-session sweeper was newly gated on the shared DurableCleanup admission class. Because the class is single-slot and shared with other cleanup loops, and because denial causes the sweep to be skipped rather than queued, abandoned stream-session cleanup can be starved.
Before this commit, the stream-session sweeper always called scavenge_abandoned_stream_sessions on each sweep interval. The new code only runs that cleanup if it can immediately acquire BackgroundWorkClass::DurableCleanup. That class has a fixed limit of 1 and is also used by object-payload reclaim and lifecycle cleanup. The admission API is non-blocking: on limit exhaustion it returns None and the stream sweeper simply waits until the next sweep interval without queuing or otherwise ensuring eventual cleanup. A workload that keeps durable cleanup occupied can therefore indefinitely postpone cleanup of abandoned PutObject stream sessions. Those sessions are security-relevant availability state: scavenging aborts stale stream uploads and releases associated object generation reservations and staged payload shards. If cleanup is starved, abandoned streaming state can accumulate and keep disk/reservation state alive longer than intended, potentially leading to service-wide availability degradation or disk exhaustion under a malicious authorized-write workload combined with cleanup pressure.

