## Report
Validated the finding. I added a targeted PoC unit test in crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs that uses the real StorageCluster and StreamSessionSweeper. The test creates an abandoned PutObject stream session, holds the single shared DurableCleanup admission permit for 16s, starts the real stream-session sweeper, verifies the stale session remains while the permit is held, then releases the permit and verifies a later sweep cleans it. Command output: `test coordinator::runtime::tests::stream_session_sweeper_skips_scavenge_while_durable_cleanup_slot_is_held ... ok ... finished in 16.77s`. I also ran the existing direct scavenger test: `scavenging_stale_sessions_removes_abandoned_streaming_state ... ok`, confirming the cleanup routine itself removes stale stream state when called. Debugger evidence: LLDB breakpoint at crates/server-core/src/coordinator/runtime.rs:142 stopped in `BackgroundWorkAdmission::try_acquire(self=..., class=DurableCleanup)` on the `denied_limit` path, with frame #1 at `StreamSessionSweeper::spawn` runtime.rs:1018. This proves the stream sweeper reaches the non-blocking admission denial path and therefore skips the body containing `scavenge_abandoned_stream_sessions`. Code evidence: DurableCleanup limit is 1 at runtime.rs:50-52; try_acquire returns None without queuing at runtime.rs:119-143; object payload reclaim also uses DurableCleanup at runtime.rs:369-374; lifecycle sweeping uses it at runtime.rs:548-552; the stream sweeper now only calls scavenge under the same permit at runtime.rs:1017-1030. The previous commit called `storage_cluster.scavenge_abandoned_stream_sessions(...)` unconditionally in the stream sweeper. The cleanup is availability-relevant because crates/storage/src/cluster.rs:6772-6810 aborts stale PutObject stream sessions and releases object generation reservations. Valgrind was attempted but unavailable in the container (`bash: command not found: valgrind`). A direct crash was not expected because this is a starvation/availability logic bug, not a memory-safety crash.

