## Narrative
The finding is a real in-scope availability bug. The commit changed the stream-session sweeper so it only calls scavenge_abandoned_stream_sessions after acquiring BackgroundWorkClass::DurableCleanup. That class has a hardcoded limit of one and is also used by object-payload reclaim and lifecycle sweeping. The admission API is non-blocking and returns None without queueing; when that occurs, the stream sweeper simply waits for the next 10 second interval. The scavenger it skips is responsible for aborting stale PutObject stream sessions and releasing their object generation reservations. Network reachability exists through the argmin-s3 HTTP S3 frontend and streaming PutObject path, but exploitation requires write authorization or a public-write bucket, creation of stale streaming state, and durable cleanup pressure. The validation evidence demonstrates the starvation behavior with the real sweeper/storage components, but not via a full remote HTTP exploit. Therefore medium availability severity is appropriate, with low-to-medium practical likelihood and no evidence of confidentiality, integrity, RCE, or authentication bypass impact.
## Controls
- SigV4 authentication and credential store
- bucket/object authorization including ACLs, bucket policy, public access block, and expected owner checks
- optional TLS when ARGMIN_TLS_CERT_PATH and ARGMIN_TLS_KEY_PATH are configured
- default listen address is loopback 127.0.0.1:9000 unless operator changes ARGMIN_LISTEN_ADDR
- connection semaphore max_connections and request semaphore max_inflight_requests
- header and body idle timeouts
- streaming checksums/content hash validation
- best-effort abort guard for normal streaming PUT failures
- local debug endpoint is disabled by default and restricted to loopback when enabled
## Blindspots
- Static-only review did not run a full remote HTTP exploit; validation evidence was a targeted component/unit test using real StorageCluster and sweeper logic.
- No deployment manifests or load balancer/IaC files were present, so internet exposure cannot be confirmed from the repository.
- Repository review cannot quantify production frequency of abandoned sessions because normal streaming paths attempt best-effort abort cleanup.
- No per-tenant quota or operational disk sizing data was available to calculate time-to-exhaustion.
- The analysis did not inspect external reverse proxy/TLS/rate-limit controls because they are outside repository artifacts.
